<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Castilian Almería</h1><p class="st-meta">1147 CE – 1157 CE · capital Almería</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The great Muslim port of Almería under Christian rule for a decade after its capture by a Castilian-led crusade in 1147.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1147 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII of Castile</a>, styled Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, led a coalition including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer IV of Barcelona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garcia-ramirez-of-navarre">García Ramírez of Navarre</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a>, then a corsair base. The city fell in October 1147, and the Genoese received a share of it.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> was held as an isolated outpost far from Castilian territory, garrisoned by Castilian troops while <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a> sought trading privileges. Much of the Muslim population had been killed, enslaved or had fled, and the economy declined. The conquest was celebrated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poem-of-almeria">Poem of Almería</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1157 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> besieged and recaptured the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII</a>, failing to relieve it, died on his way home. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> remained Muslim until 1489, when it surrendered to the Catholic Monarchs.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1147 CE – 1157 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.53%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1147 CE – 1157 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Almería</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Alfonso VII</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Castilian, Andalusi Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Almería under Alfonso VII</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1152&amp;focus=castilian-almeria" aria-label="Castilian Almería on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4618 850 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Castilian Almería lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4938 1106L4938 1106M4938 1106L4865 919M4938 1106L4794 1282"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4938 1106h0M4865 919h0M4794 1282h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4938 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4938 1106h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Almería. <a href="/atlas?year=1152&amp;focus=castilian-almeria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

